//
// Normative rule hstatus_gva_op (src/priv/hypervisor.adoc):
//   Field GVA (Guest Virtual Address) is written by the implementation whenever a trap is taken
//     into HS-mode. For any trap (breakpoint, address misaligned, access fault, page fault, or
//     guest-page fault) that writes a guest virtual address to stval, GVA is set to 1. For any
//     other trap into HS-mode, GVA is set to 0.
//
// Context:
//   A guest virtual address comes from a VS/VU access or from HLV/HSV (whose effective mode is VS
//     or VU) in any mode. Breakpoints, illegal, virtual-instruction and ecall traps from HS/U, and
//     illegal, virtual and ecall from VS/VU, write no guest virtual address.
//
// Clarifications:
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/672 and https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-
//     manual/issues/673: breakpoint and address-misaligned traps that write a guest virtual address
//     also set GVA. https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/2695 (aswaterman): "Since HLV
//     etc. always have effective privilege VU or VS, their virtual addresses are always guest
//     virtual addresses, and so GVA is set to 1." Whether writing stval=0 sets GVA is not settled,
//     so GVA is checked only when xtval is nonzero.
//
// Test plan:
//   1. GVA preset 0, then a guest-virtual-address trap: ebreak, load, store and fetch access faults
//     from VS and VU; HLV.W and HSV.W access faults from HS-mode and from U-mode with hstatus.HU=1:
//     GVA = 1 (checked when stval is nonzero).
//   2. GVA preset 1, then a trap without one: ebreak and load fault from HS and U; illegal, ecall
//     from VS, VU, U; virtual-instruction from VS and VU: GVA = 0.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 7259
//

